::SPEECH_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which speech plan is most complete?
{
~A. Topic only: presentations.#A topic alone is incomplete because it does not include focus, points, support, or transitions.
=B. Focus, three related points, support, reasoning, transitions, and conclusion.#Correct. This plan includes the major parts needed for an integrated informative speech.
~C. Three unrelated facts about school.#Unrelated facts do not create a coherent informative speech plan.
~D. A full paragraph to read aloud.#A full script may prevent natural speech delivery and does not show planning parts clearly.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Point: Organized notes improve delivery. Which support best fits?
{
~A. Some notes use blue ink.#This detail does not explain how notes improve delivery.
~B. Delivery is part of speaking.#This repeats the topic without supporting the point.
=C. Notes grouped by main point help the speaker find the next idea quickly.#Correct. This support explains how organized notes improve delivery.
~D. Some speeches are short.#This does not support the point about organized notes.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Practicing aloud helps speakers notice unclear sections before presenting. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
=A. Rehearsing out loud can help speakers find confusing parts before the presentation.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and changes the wording.
~B. Practicing aloud makes every speech perfect.#This exaggerates the source and changes the meaning.
~C. Speakers should never revise unclear sections.#This contradicts the source meaning.
~D. Presenting is the same as reading silently.#This adds an inaccurate idea that is not in the source.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which note is most speech-ready?
{
~A. Say everything about speeches.#This is too vague to guide delivery.
~B. Read the full source paragraph.#This may lead to reading instead of presenting.
~C. Random speaking ideas.#This does not follow a useful format.
=D. Point 1: outline order; support: steps help audience follow.#Correct. This note gives a clear point and support cue.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which transition best connects organization to support?
{
~A. Next thing.#This does not show how the ideas connect.
=B. After organizing the main points, the speaker needs support that explains each point.#Correct. This transition shows a clear relationship between planning and support.
~C. I am changing to something else.#This signals a shift but does not connect ideas.
~D. Anyway, support.#This is too casual and unclear for informative speaking.
}
